SYMBOL TABLES, ADDRESS SPACES AND OFFSETS

The kernel exposes symbol catalogs under /proc/sym. These are
NOT automatically mapped onto every executing address space.

EXAMINE
  ls /proc/sym
  grep pwm /proc/sym/kernel
  mem -s kernel
  mem -s sh

KERNEL VS USER
  Kernel symbols may be absolute XIP Flash addresses (0x100...)
  or absolute SRAM addresses (0x200...). Compare only with the
  exact running firmware image; rebuilds change addresses.
  User app symbols are slot-relative offsets; add the actual
  loaded slot base only after identifying the program's slot.
  'mem -s sh' shows a symbol list; plain 'mem' is interactive.

HOW TO CORRELATE
  1. Use ps / memory map to identify the task and region.
  2. Use mem -s APP for the app symbol offset.
  3. Confirm the actual task slot load base.
  4. Add offset to slot base, if this is the format in the current
     app catalog; then inspect a short, valid memory range.
  5. For kernel XIP symbols, compare an address from the trace or
     firmware map against /proc/sym/kernel. Do not assume every
     trace function name supplies a program counter.

WARNINGS
  Duplicate function names can appear in catalogs from different
  build units. A name match alone is not a unique location.
  A disassembly of Thumb code requires correct halfword alignment
  and Thumb instruction encoding. Raw hexadecimal bytes are not
  automatically instruction text.
